                <h2>Step 1: Account Initialization – Establish the Financial Baseline</h2>
<p>After opening a new overseas advertising account, the operations team must complete the following initialization steps:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Bind Settlement Information</strong>: Link credit cards, bank accounts, or payment tools (such as Payoneer or PingPong) to the advertising platform backend to ensure smooth recharge channels.</li>
<li><strong>Set Budget Caps</strong>: Configure daily or total budget limits for each account based on the campaign plan to prevent unexpected overspending.</li>
<li><strong>Record Account Ledgers</strong>: Create account files in the internal financial system, documenting platform, account ID, currency type, and bound payment methods.</li>
<li><strong>Configure Notification Channels</strong>: Set up push notifications for account balance changes and spending alerts to ensure timely delivery to operations leads.</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p>Recommendation: Confirm currency types before the first recharge. Some platforms (e.g., Google Ads) support multi-currency accounts—evaluate potential exchange rate losses before switching.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Step 2: Balance Monitoring – A Three-Tier Alert System</h2>
<p>Establishing a clear balance alert mechanism is critical to preventing ad delivery interruptions. Consider setting the following three alert levels:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Yellow Alert (Balance ≤ 3 Days' Spend)</strong>: Operations staff receive a reminder and begin evaluating whether a recharge is needed while verifying payment method status.</li>
<li><strong>Orange Alert (Balance ≤ 1 Day's Spend)</strong>: Initiate the recharge process, prioritizing verified payment methods, and notify the finance department to prepare standby funds.</li>
<li><strong>Red Alert (Balance ≤ 6 Hours' Spend)</strong>: Execute the recharge immediately. If delays occur, pause high-spend ad groups to prevent rapid fund depletion.</li>
</ul>
<p>Operations staff can use platform built-in automated rules (e.g., Meta's "pause campaigns when balance falls below threshold") as supplementary tools, but should not rely solely on automated processing.</p>
<h2>Step 3: Recharge Workflow – Standardized Procedures</h2>
<h3>3.1 Credit/Debit Card Recharge</h3>
<ol>
<li>Log into the advertising platform backend and navigate to "Billing &amp; Payments."</li>
<li>Select "Add Payment Method" and bind a valid credit or debit card.</li>
<li>Enter the recharge amount (note minimum and maximum recharge limits).</li>
<li>Confirm payment and wait for platform processing (typically 5-30 minutes for arrival).</li>
<li>Record the recharge amount, time, and transaction reference number in the internal ledger.</li>
</ol>
<h3>3.2 Payment Tool Recharge (Payoneer / PingPong)</h3>
<ol>
<li>Verify the payment tool balance is sufficient and platform qualification certification is completed.</li>
<li>Select the corresponding payment tool as the payment method in the advertising platform backend.</li>
<li>Complete authorization and recharge operations following platform instructions.</li>
<li>Some platforms support direct debit mode—configure deduction rules in advance.</li>
</ol>
<h3>3.3 Bank Transfer Recharge</h3>
<p>Bank transfers typically require 2-5 business days to arrive and are suitable for large-value recharge scenarios. Plan ahead to avoid selecting this method when immediate delivery is needed.</p>
<h2>Step 4: Multi-Account Fund Coordination – Priority and Quota Management</h2>
<p>When managing multiple overseas advertising accounts, fund allocation priority directly impacts delivery performance. Follow these principles:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Allocate by Campaign Phase</strong>: Maintain lower balances for testing-phase accounts and reserve sufficient funds for scaled accounts.</li>
<li><strong>Set Account Quotas</strong>: Establish monthly recharge limits per account to prevent a single account from over-consuming the overall budget.</li>
<li><strong>Establish Transfer Mechanisms</strong>: When an account runs low on balance and immediate recharge is unavailable, temporary transfers from lower-priority accounts can be arranged (confirm the process with the finance department in advance).</li>
</ul>
<h2>Step 5: Financial Reconciliation – Daily Clearing and Monthly Settlement</h2>
<h3>Daily Reconciliation</h3>
<p>At a fixed time each day (e.g., 10:00 AM), verify each account's previous-day spend data against the platform backend reports. Confirm data consistency and enter it into the financial system.</p>
<h3>Monthly Settlement</h3>
<p>At the beginning of each month, complete the previous month's account fund summary, including: total recharge amount, total spend, remaining balance, and actual CAC (Customer Acquisition Cost). Generate a fund utilization efficiency report and submit it to campaign leads and finance supervisors.</p>
<h2>Step 6: Risk Control Framework</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Payment Method Backup</strong>: Bind at least two payment methods per account. When the primary channel fails, switch to the backup channel.</li>
<li><strong>Permission-Based Access Control</strong>: Recharge operations require dual authorization to avoid single-point operational risks. Finance staff have view-only access while campaign staff have operational permissions.</li>
<li><strong>Audit Log Retention</strong>: Record all recharge and account change operations—time, operator, amount, and transaction ID—retained for at least 12 months.</li>
<li><strong>Anomaly Monitoring</strong>: If recharge non-delivery or abnormal balance decline is detected, immediately contact the platform's official customer service and notify the finance department.</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<p><strong>Execution Checklist</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Account initialization completed; ledger established</li>
<li>[ ] Three-tier alert system configured and tested</li>
<li>[ ] Payment methods bound and verified</li>
<li>[ ] Daily reconciliation scheduled into work calendar</li>
<li>[ ] Risk control permissions分级 set</li>
</ul>
